## 🚀 Getting Started & Third-Party Setup

Follow these steps to set up the external API keys, storage buckets, and database configurations required to compile and run the application locally.

### 1. Secure Environment Setup (`local.properties`)
To keep API keys secure, this project uses the `local.properties` file to inject credentials via `BuildConfig`. **The app will not compile without this file.**

1. In Android Studio, navigate to the root directory of the project.
2. Open (or create) the `local.properties` file.
3. Add the following keys (you will generate these in the steps below):

```properties
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=your_cloud_name_here
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=your_api_secret_here
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_key_here
Note: Do not use quotes around the values in this file.

2. Firebase Configuration (Auth & Database)
Firebase provides user authentication and real-time backend updates.

Project Initialization:

Go to the Firebase Console and create a new project.

Register a new Android App using the exact package name: com.example.wardeobe.

Download the generated google-services.json file.

Move google-services.json into the app/ directory of your cloned repository.

Authentication Setup:

In the Firebase Console, go to Build > Authentication.

Click Get Started, navigate to the Sign-in method tab.

Select Email/Password, enable it, and save.

Firestore Database Setup:

Go to Build > Firestore Database and create your database instance.

Navigate to the Rules tab and modify them to allow authenticated read/write access:

JavaScript
rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /users/{userId} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == userId;
    }
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
3. Cloudinary Setup (Media Management)
Cloudinary is used to host and serve uploaded media files such as profile images and wardrobe items.

Register or log in at Cloudinary.

Go to your Dashboard to find your Cloud Name, API Key, and API Secret.

Add these credentials to your local.properties file.

Go to the Media Library tab and create a dedicated folder (e.g., wardrobe_uploads) to organize your app's assets.

4. Google AI Studio (Gemini Image Generation)
This project uses the Gemini API (`gemini-3.1-flash-image`) to handle AI tasks like virtual try-on image generation.

Go to Google AI Studio (https://aistudio.google.com/) and log in.

Navigate to Get API Key and create a new API Key for your project.

Add this key to your local.properties file as `GEMINI_API_KEY`.

**IMPORTANT**: While Google AI Studio offers a free tier (e.g., 500 image generations per day/month), image generation models generally require a billing account (credit card) to be attached to your Google Cloud / AI Studio account to verify your identity and prevent abuse. If you do not have a billing method attached, your API quota for image models will be set to `0`, and you will receive a `429 Quota Exceeded` error when the app tries to generate an image.

5. Build & Run
Once your local.properties and google-services.json files are in place:

Open the project in Android Studio.

Sync the project with Gradle files (this generates the BuildConfig class).

Build and run the app on your emulator or physical device.

Ensure your app-level build.gradle.kts includes the necessary plugins (like the Google Services plugin) to support Firebase services, networking, and image loading.


***

### A Quick Tip Before You Commit:
Just remember to double-check that your `local.properties` file is listed inside your root `.gitignore`file before you push this to GitHub, so your new keys stay safe!